WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.474
This chapter is all about heliox, a mixture of helium and oxygen.

2
00:00:05.474 --> 00:00:17.182
We start with the three mixtures the deck lists, why helium makes breathing easier, and why the mixture must always carry at least twenty percent oxygen.

3
00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:24.681
Then the delivery methods, for patients who breathe on their own and for patients on a ventilator.

4
00:00:24.681 --> 00:00:30.803
Next is the flowmeter correction factor, worked through with the deck's example.

5
00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:38.762
We finish with heliox for infants, and the separate flow increase needed when heliox drives a nebulizer.

6
00:00:38.762 --> 00:00:43.200
Keep those two corrections apart; they are easy to mix up.

7
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:57.386
Most centers use premixed cylinders in one of three combinations: eighty percent helium with twenty percent oxygen, seventy with thirty, and sixty with forty.

8
00:00:57.386 --> 00:01:13.167
Heliox decreases the work of breathing, especially in large airway obstruction or severe asthma, because helium is much less dense than nitrogen, so it flows more easily through narrowed airways.

9
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:17.537
Always use helium with at least twenty percent oxygen.

10
00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:31.213
Tanks of pure helium exist, but they need a blending system, and if the oxygen source failed, the patient could get one hundred percent helium and quickly become anoxic.

11
00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:34.450
That is why premixed cylinders are used.

12
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:41.923
Heliox is not a replacement for oxygen therapy in a hypoxemic patient.

13
00:01:41.923 --> 00:01:53.691
Hess adds that if an FiO2 above point four zero is needed, the limited helium left in the mix is unlikely to produce a clinical benefit.

14
00:01:53.691 --> 00:02:06.150
Hess names eighty twenty and seventy thirty as the most popular mixtures, and chapter thirty two uses sixty to eighty percent helium for asthma.

15
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:20.600
The sources differ slightly: the deck lists three mixtures, eighty twenty, seventy thirty and sixty forty, while its notes and Hess name only the first two as typical.

16
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.417
Use the deck's three.

17
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:36.099
For a spontaneously breathing patient, the deck uses a tight-fitting nonrebreathing mask at a flow that meets inspiratory demand, or a high-flow cannula.

18
00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:45.318
For a mechanically ventilated patient, heliox goes through a cuffed artificial airway, or by the noninvasive route.

19
00:02:45.318 --> 00:02:53.094
Through a flowmeter, use one calibrated for that helium percentage, or apply a correction factor.

20
00:02:53.094 --> 00:03:01.913
Monitor every patient closely with an oxygen analyzer that measures the FiO2 continuously, with active alarms.

21
00:03:01.913 --> 00:03:15.140
Hess adds a well-fitting simple mask or a reservoir mask with a Y-piece for aerosolized drugs, often at twelve to fifteen liters per minute to keep the bag inflated.

22
00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:22.035
A tightly sealed, closed system is required, because helium leaks through small holes.

23
00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:28.608
On a ventilator, helium can alter the delivered and measured exhaled tidal volume.

24
00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:39.970
An oxygen flowmeter under-reads heliox flow, so multiply the indicated flow by the correction factor to get the actual flow.

25
00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:49.426
The factor is one point eight for eighty twenty, one point six for seventy thirty, and one point four for sixty forty.

26
00:03:49.426 --> 00:03:54.475
The more helium, the larger the factor, and the lower the FiO2.

27
00:03:54.475 --> 00:04:09.059
The deck's example: ten liters per minute indicated on an oxygen flowmeter delivering eighty twenty is ten times one point eight, or eighteen liters per minute of actual heliox flow.

28
00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:20.999
Hess gives only the eighty twenty factor, one point eight, from Graham's law: eighty twenty heliox diffuses one point eight times faster than oxygen.

29
00:04:20.999 --> 00:04:29.333
Hess's one point eight zero five and one point five eight six are density ratios, not flowmeter factors.

30
00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:32.462
Pause and calculate.

31
00:04:32.462 --> 00:04:40.931
You want fifteen liters per minute of eighty twenty heliox, and you only have an oxygen flowmeter.

32
00:04:40.931 --> 00:04:43.783
What do you set the flowmeter to?

33
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:50.700
[6-second practice pause]

34
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.153
Reverse the correction.

35
00:04:53.153 --> 00:05:03.514
The actual flow is the indicated flow times one point eight, so the setting is the wanted flow divided by one point eight.

36
00:05:03.514 --> 00:05:10.308
Fifteen divided by one point eight is about eight point three liters per minute.

37
00:05:10.308 --> 00:05:19.650
Set eight point three, and the flowmeter will deliver about fifteen liters per minute of eighty twenty heliox.

38
00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:46.114
The chapter twelve deck repeats the delivery methods: a tight-fitting nonrebreathing mask from premixed cylinders, at a flow that meets or exceeds the patient's minute ventilation and peak inspiratory flow, or a high-flow nasal cannula, and for invasive ventilation, a cuffed tracheal airway with a heliox-capable ventilator.

39
00:05:46.114 --> 00:05:52.665
For infants with bronchiolitis, heliox can be given by oxyhood, but there is a catch.

40
00:05:52.665 --> 00:06:05.073
Helium's high thermal conductivity causes heat loss and hypothermia, which has limited oxyhood use in this group, and a high-flow nasal cannula is now preferred.

41
00:06:05.073 --> 00:06:10.160
Helium carries heat away from the infant far better than air does.

42
00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:18.792
So if a question offers heliox by oxyhood for an infant, the better answer is usually a high-flow nasal cannula.

43
00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:40.615
Heliox has been combined with jet-nebulized bronchodilators to treat severe acute obstruction, such as status asthmaticus, especially when standard therapy fails, and nebulizing with heliox may improve how well the drug is delivered and deposited.

44
00:06:40.615 --> 00:06:48.385
But the flow must be increased by fifty to one hundred percent when heliox powers a nebulizer.

45
00:06:48.385 --> 00:07:03.097
Heliox's low density makes the nebulizer less efficient at lower flows, which lowers the inhaled dose, and the higher flow restores the inhaled drug and the respirable particles.

46
00:07:03.097 --> 00:07:05.659
Keep the two corrections apart.

47
00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:12.106
The flowmeter factor corrects a reading: you multiply to find the actual flow.

48
00:07:12.106 --> 00:07:15.495
The nebulizer increase changes a setting.

49
00:07:15.495 --> 00:07:20.950
In both cases, monitor with an oxygen analyzer with active alarms.

50
00:07:22.367 --> 00:07:23.214
One more.

51
00:07:23.214 --> 00:07:30.935
An infant with bronchiolitis is ordered heliox, and the order suggests an oxyhood.

52
00:07:30.935 --> 00:07:36.867
What is the concern, and what delivery method is now preferred?

53
00:07:37.767 --> 00:07:43.767
[6-second practice pause]

54
00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:47.690
The concern is heat loss and hypothermia.

55
00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:57.041
Helium's high thermal conductivity carries heat away from the infant, and in a hood the infant sits in that gas.

56
00:07:57.041 --> 00:08:05.641
That is why oxyhood use has been limited in this group, and a high-flow nasal cannula is now preferred.

57
00:08:05.641 --> 00:08:10.567
Monitor the oxygen with an analyzer that has active alarms.

58
00:08:11.967 --> 00:08:14.075
Here is what to remember.

59
00:08:14.075 --> 00:08:23.772
Heliox comes premixed as eighty twenty, seventy thirty and sixty forty, always with at least twenty percent oxygen.

60
00:08:23.772 --> 00:08:31.867
It lowers the work of breathing because helium is much less dense, but it is not oxygen therapy.

61
00:08:31.867 --> 00:08:42.914
Give it through a tight-fitting nonrebreather, a high-flow cannula, or a cuffed airway, in a sealed system with an oxygen analyzer.

62
00:08:42.914 --> 00:08:54.382
An oxygen flowmeter under-reads heliox, so multiply by one point eight, one point six or one point four, and divide to find the setting.

63
00:08:54.382 --> 00:09:05.092
Infants get a high-flow cannula rather than a hood, and a heliox-driven nebulizer needs fifty to one hundred percent more flow.
